    "stem": "Sagar knows $\\mathrm{HCF}(9,15)=3$. He multiplies both numbers by $4$ to get $36$ and $60$.\n\nWhich claim about $\\mathrm{HCF}(36,60)$ is correct?",
    "item_type": "mcq",
    "options": {
      "A": "The shared factor $4$ joins the old common factor $3$, so $\\mathrm{HCF}(36,60)=4 \\times 3 = 12$.",
      "B": "Call the answer the common multiplier $4$ alone, so $\\mathrm{HCF}(36,60)=4$.",
      "C": "After multiplying by $4$, the HCF is still $3$, with no new common factor to include.",
      "D": "The HCF is $60$, because $60$ is the larger printed number."
